const pages = {
  '/': {
    title: 'Aviation Facilities Knowledge | Pondco Online',
    description: 'Plain-language resources for understanding aviation-facility planning, procurement, and project delivery.',
    eyebrow: 'Aviation facilities knowledge base',
    heading: 'Clear context for complex aviation-facility projects.',
    intro: 'Pondco Online publishes practical, plainly written background material for people evaluating aviation-facility planning and delivery. It is an independent information resource, not a substitute for professional engineering, legal, procurement, or safety advice.',
    sections: [
      ['Start with the project question', 'Every airport project begins with constraints: operational need, site conditions, funding rules, schedule, and stakeholder responsibilities. Defining those constraints early makes later design and procurement decisions easier to evaluate.'],
      ['Understand the delivery path', 'Public infrastructure work often moves through discovery, feasibility, scope definition, procurement, design, construction, and closeout. Each phase produces different records and decisions; keeping them traceable helps teams communicate clearly.'],
      ['Use independent review', 'A qualified team should validate technical assumptions, regulatory obligations, and site-specific risks before a project proceeds. Readers should consult the appropriate licensed professionals and public authorities for decisions affecting an active facility.'],
    ],
  },
  '/services': {
    title: 'Aviation Project Delivery Topics | Pondco Online',
    description: 'An overview of common topics in aviation facility project delivery: scoping, design coordination, procurement, construction, and closeout.',
    eyebrow: 'Project delivery topics',
    heading: 'A practical map of the work behind aviation facilities.',
    intro: 'Pondco Online publishes background material on how aviation-facility projects are planned and delivered. The pages below explain the major phases in plain language: what each phase is for, who is typically involved, and ',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Pondco Online publishes background material on how aviation-facility projects are planned and delivered. The pages below explain the major phases in plain language: what each phase is for, who is typically involved, and which records and decisions it produces. This is educational material, not engineering, legal, or procurement advice.'],
      ['Planning and scope', 'Every airport project starts with a problem that needs a clear statement. A useful scope identifies the operating problem, the desired outcome, the decision-makers, the known constraints, and the information still missing. Examples of operating problems include a terminal that cannot handle peak passenger loads, a runway pavement section approaching the end of its service life, or a fuel farm that no longer meets current standards. Clear scope language is the foundation of a defensible procurement process. When the scope is vague, bids are hard to compare, change orders multiply, and disputes about what was included become likely. A strong scope describes what is in, what is out, the performance the owner expects, and the standards that apply. It also names the constraints that will shape the work: budget limits, funding rules, schedule windows, operational requirements, environmental obligations, and stakeholder approvals. Feasibility work usually comes before detailed design. A feasibility study tests whether the project is worth pursuing by comparing options against cost, schedule, risk, and benefit. For public airports, feasibility often feeds into planning documents that funding agencies require, such as an airport layout plan update or a master plan element. Keeping the feasibility assumptions written down matters because they become the baseline the rest of the project is measured against.'],
      ['Design coordination', 'Design coordination connects civil, structural, mechanical, electrical, communications, security, and operational requirements into one buildable set of documents. On an aviation project the list of disciplines is long because the facility itself is complex: airfield pavements, drainage, airfield lighting and signage, navigational aids, terminal buildings, baggage systems, security screening areas, fueling, utilities, roadways, and parking all have to work together. Coordination happens in stages. Early design sets the concept and confirms it against the program and budget. Developed design resolves the systems and their interfaces. Construction documents translate those decisions into drawings and specifications a contractor can price and build. At each stage, someone has to check that the pieces fit: that the structural engineer knows where the baggage system needs openings, that the electrical engineer has accounted for the airfield lighting loads, that the civil engineer has coordinated grading with the building foundations. The right disciplines and approvals depend on the facility and jurisdiction. Aviation projects typically involve the airport owner, the airline tenants, the Federal Aviation Administration for airspace and design standards, state aviation agencies, local permitting authorities, and sometimes the Transportation Security Administration for screening areas. Each party has its own review cycles, and the design schedule has to accommodate them.'],
      ['Procurement', 'Public airport work is usually procured under rules that vary by state, funding source, and project size. Common delivery methods include design-bid-build, where the owner contracts separately with a designer and a builder; design-build, where one entity handles both; and construction manager at risk, where a construction manager joins during design and commits to a guaranteed maximum price. Each method distributes risk and control differently, and the choice affects everything from the design schedule to how changes are priced. Whatever method is used, the procurement record matters. Solicitation documents, addenda, bid tabulations, evaluation scoring, and award decisions form the paper trail that demonstrates a fair process. For federally funded work, grant assurances and Buy American requirements add another layer of compliance that the procurement documents must reflect.'],
      ['Construction and closeout', 'During construction, teams generally track submittals, changes, inspections, testing, commissioning, records, and handover requirements. Submittals are the contractor\'s proposed materials and equipment, reviewed against the specifications before installation. Requests for information resolve ambiguities in the documents. Change orders document agreed adjustments to scope, price, or schedule. Inspections and testing verify the work meets the specified standards. On an active airfield, construction phasing is a project in itself. Work has to be sequenced so the airport keeps operating safely: runways and taxiways may close in segments, haul routes must stay clear of aircraft movement areas, and temporary lighting and signage must meet standards every night. A phasing plan coordinates all of this and is typically reviewed by airport operations and the FAA before work begins. Closeout is where many projects stumble. The punch list identifies incomplete or defective work. Commissioning verifies that building systems perform as designed. Operation and maintenance manuals, warranties, spare parts, training, and record drawings all have to be delivered before the owner can properly run the facility. The governing contract and the authority having jurisdiction establish the actual process, and readers involved in a real project should follow those documents rather than any general summary.'],
      ['How to use this material', 'These topics are written for readers who are evaluating, planning, or learning about aviation-facility work: airport staff, consultants, contractors, students, and members of the public trying to understand a project in their community. Start with the project question, understand the delivery path, and insist on independent review by qualified professionals before making decisions that affect an active facility.'],
    ],
  },
  '/markets': {
    title: 'Markets & Context | Pondco Online',
    description: 'How aviation facility projects intersect with operational continuity, public accountability, stakeholders, stewardship, and funding.',
    eyebrow: 'Markets and context',
    heading: 'The forces that shape aviation facility work.',
    intro: 'Airport and public-facility projects connect operators, communities, public agencies, consultants, contractors, and funding bodies. No single party controls the whole effort, which is why infrastructure decisions are coo',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Airport and public-facility projects connect operators, communities, public agencies, consultants, contractors, and funding bodies. No single party controls the whole effort, which is why infrastructure decisions are coordination decisions. This page explains the recurring themes that shape public aviation projects.'],
      ['Operational continuity', 'Facilities are often improved while normal operations continue. An airport cannot simply close for a year while its runway is rebuilt or its terminal expanded; airlines, passengers, cargo operators, and general aviation all depend on it staying open. Planning should account for access, phasing, communications, safety controls, and contingency planning. Phasing plans divide the work into segments that can be built while the rest of the facility operates. On an airfield this might mean reconstructing a runway in halves, shifting traffic to a parallel taxiway, or working at night when flight activity is lightest. In a terminal it might mean renovating one concourse while passengers use another. Each phase needs its own safety plan, its own temporary signage and lighting, and its own communication to tenants and the traveling public. Contingency planning covers what happens when the plan meets reality: a weather delay, an unexpected subsurface condition, a material shortage, or a schedule slip that pushes work into a busier season. Projects that plan for disruption explicitly tend to recover faster than projects that treat the baseline schedule as a promise.'],
      ['Public accountability', 'Public projects benefit from clear records of scope, selection, changes, and outcomes. Taxpayers and elected officials have a legitimate interest in how money is spent, and funding agencies require documentation as a condition of their grants. Requirements vary by jurisdiction, funding source, and procurement method, but the principle is consistent: decisions should be traceable. Traceability starts with the scope and the procurement record — what was asked for, who was selected, and why. It continues through design decisions, change orders, inspection results, and the final accounting of cost and schedule against the original plan. When records are complete and accessible, questions from auditors, journalists, and the public can be answered with documents rather than recollections. Transparency also has practical value during the project. Regular, honest status reporting — what is done, what is next, what has changed, and what it costs — builds trust with stakeholders and gives decision-makers the information they need to act early when problems appear.'],
      ['Stakeholder landscape', 'A typical public airport project involves more parties than a newcomer expects. The airport owner sets the program and holds the contracts. Airlines and other tenants have operational needs and lease rights that constrain the work. The FAA sets design standards and controls airspace. State aviation agencies may provide funding and oversight. Local governments handle permits and land use. The TSA governs security screening areas. Utility companies, environmental regulators, and community groups each have their own standing. Each stakeholder has different authority, different timelines, and different priorities. Coordination means mapping who needs to approve what, in which sequence, and building the project schedule around those approvals rather than discovering them late. Early stakeholder identification is one of the cheapest risk-reduction measures available.'],
      ['Long-term stewardship', 'Design choices affect maintainability, resilience, accessibility, and operating cost for years. A documented lifecycle perspective helps decision-makers weigh immediate and future tradeoffs. The cheapest option to build is rarely the cheapest option to own, and public owners live with their facilities for decades. Lifecycle thinking shows up in concrete choices: pavement sections designed for the actual aircraft mix rather than the minimum standard, building systems selected for maintainability and parts availability, drainage designed for the storms the region actually gets, and materials chosen for the local climate. It also shows up in records: as-built drawings, equipment manuals, and maintenance histories that let future staff understand what was built and why. Resilience deserves explicit attention. Airports are critical infrastructure, and their facilities face weather, flooding, heat, and evolving security requirements. Designing with those stresses in mind — and documenting the assumptions — gives future operators a head start when conditions change.'],
      ['Funding context', 'Most public airport capital work blends multiple funding sources: federal grants, state grants, passenger facility charges, airport revenue, and sometimes bonds. Each source has its own eligibility rules, timing, and reporting obligations. A project that is eligible under one program may not be under another, and the funding mix often determines the project schedule as much as the construction logic does. Understanding the funding context early prevents the common failure of designing a project that the available money cannot pay for in the sequence it arrives.'],
    ],
  },
  '/portfolio': {
    title: 'Evaluating Project Information | Pondco Online',
    description: 'How to read aviation project information critically: primary sources, dates and scope, and illustrations.',
    eyebrow: 'Project information',
    heading: 'How to evaluate aviation project information.',
    intro: 'Airport projects generate a flood of information: press releases, renderings, status dashboards, board presentations, and social media updates. Some of it is authoritative and some of it is aspirational. This page offers',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Airport projects generate a flood of information: press releases, renderings, status dashboards, board presentations, and social media updates. Some of it is authoritative and some of it is aspirational. This page offers a practical method for sorting one from the other.'],
      ['Look for primary sources', 'Where available, use public owner records, procurement notices, permits, and official agency publications to corroborate material claims. A press release saying a project "broke ground" is a claim; the issued notice to proceed is the record. A rendering of a future terminal is a concept; the approved airport layout plan is the document the FAA actually recognizes. Primary sources for airport projects commonly include: Procurement records. Solicitations, bid tabulations, and award notices show what was actually bought, from whom, and for how much. Most public owners publish these. Board and commission materials. Meeting agendas, staff reports, and minutes record the decisions and the reasoning behind them, including budget approvals and change orders. Permits and environmental documents. These show what was authorized and under what conditions, which often differs from what was originally proposed. FAA records. Airport layout plan approvals, grant awards, and airspace determinations are public and authoritative for federally obligated airports. Financial disclosures. Audited financial statements and capital improvement programs show how projects were actually funded and what they cost. When a claim cannot be traced to a primary source, treat it as unverified rather than false — and keep looking.'],
      ['Check dates and scope', 'A project title alone rarely explains its current status. "Terminal expansion" could describe a project in planning, in design, under construction, or finished five years ago. Confirm the date, location, phase, delivery method, and the party responsible for each statement before relying on it. Dates matter because projects evolve. A cost figure from a 2019 planning study is not the budget for the 2024 construction contract. A schedule from a grant application is not the contractor\'s current projection. Always ask: when was this written, which phase does it describe, and has anything superseded it? Scope matters because project names get reused. An airport may have a "Runway 9-27 rehabilitation" in its five-year plan for three consecutive cycles, each referring to a different segment or a deferred version of the same work. The phase and the limits of work — which pavement section, which building area, which systems — are what make a project description meaningful.'],
      ['Treat illustrations carefully', 'Concept images, diagrams, schedules, and status graphics may be illustrative. They should not be understood as regulatory approval, final design, or an operational commitment without supporting documentation. Renderings are the most common source of confusion. An architectural rendering is a visualization of a design concept, often produced before the design is complete and sometimes before funding is secured. It is not a promise that the building will look exactly that way, and it is certainly not evidence of permits or approvals. Enjoy renderings for what they are — communication tools — and verify the underlying status separately. Status dashboards and progress graphics deserve the same skepticism. A dashboard is only as reliable as its data pipeline: who updates it, how often, and from which source. A "75% complete" indicator means little without knowing whether it measures time elapsed, money spent, or physical quantities installed — three numbers that rarely agree.'],
      ['Watch for common distortions', 'A few patterns recur in project communication. Announcements emphasize starts and completions but rarely mention deferrals or descoping. Cost figures may exclude soft costs like design, inspection, and financing, or may be quoted in different year\'s dollars without saying so. Schedules may show the contractual completion date while the realistic forecast has quietly moved. None of this is necessarily dishonest — communication is condensed by nature — but it means the headline version of a project is almost always simpler than the documented version.'],
      ['A simple verification routine', 'For any significant project claim, work through these questions: Who said it, and what is their role? When was it said, and which phase does it describe? Can I find the underlying record — the contract, the approval, the permit, the board action? Does the scope described match the scope in the record? If the answer to the last two is yes, you have something solid. If not, you have a lead worth following, not a fact worth citing.'],
      ['Why this matters', 'Good information hygiene protects everyone involved. Owners make better decisions when they work from records rather than impressions. Contractors bid more accurately when scopes are verifiable. Communities participate more constructively when they can see what is actually planned. And journalists, researchers, and students produce better work when their sources are primary. The extra hour spent verifying a claim is almost always cheaper than the cost of acting on a wrong one.'],
    ],
  },
  '/faq': {
    title: 'Frequently Asked Questions | Pondco Online',
    description: 'Plain-language answers about airport improvement funding, master plans, commissioning, costs, and timelines.',
    eyebrow: 'FAQ',
    heading: 'Frequently asked questions.',
    intro: 'No. This site provides educational background material. Decisions affecting a real project should be made with the appropriate qualified and licensed professionals — engineers, architects, attorneys, and procurement spec',
    sections: [
      ['Is this professional advice?', 'No. This site provides educational background material. Decisions affecting a real project should be made with the appropriate qualified and licensed professionals — engineers, architects, attorneys, and procurement specialists — and with the public authorities that have jurisdiction over the facility.'],
      ['How can I verify a project claim?', 'Start with the relevant public owner, agency, procurement record, or project contact. Do not rely solely on a marketing summary, a rendering, or a status graphic. Our page on evaluating project information walks through a practical verification routine: find the primary source, check the date and scope, and treat illustrations as illustrative.'],
      ['Why do schedules change?', 'Schedules can be affected by funding, permitting, design coordination, procurement, weather, site conditions, materials, approvals, and operational constraints. On an active airfield, operational constraints are often the dominant factor — work windows are limited, and safety requirements can pause work with little notice. A schedule is a plan, not a promise; the useful question is usually what has changed since the baseline and why.'],
      ['What is an airport layout plan?', 'The airport layout plan is the FAA-recognized drawing set showing existing and planned airport facilities. For federally obligated airports, development shown on the approved layout plan is generally a precondition for federal funding eligibility. It is a planning document, not a construction document — it shows what is intended, not the detailed design.'],
      ['What is the difference between a master plan and a construction project?', 'A master plan is a long-range planning study that evaluates an airport\'s needs over roughly twenty years and recommends a development program. A construction project is the actual delivery of one piece of that program — design, procurement, building, and closeout. Many master plan recommendations are never built, deferred, or substantially changed before construction. Do not read a master plan as a commitment to build.'],
      ['What does "AIP" mean?', 'AIP is the FAA\'s Airport Improvement Program, the federal grant program that funds eligible airport planning and development. AIP grants come with grant assurances — obligations the airport sponsor accepts, such as keeping the airport open to public use and not discriminating among aeronautical users. Projects using AIP money must follow federal procurement and compliance requirements.'],
      ['Who approves construction on an airport?', 'It depends on the work and the jurisdiction, but the list commonly includes the airport owner, the FAA (for airspace, design standards, and layout plan consistency), state aviation agencies, local building and permitting authorities, and the TSA for security-related areas. Utility providers and environmental regulators may also have approval roles. The project team maps these approvals early because each one has its own timeline.'],
      ['What is commissioning?', 'Commissioning is the structured process of verifying that building systems — HVAC, electrical, plumbing, fire protection, baggage handling, controls — are installed correctly and perform as designed. It includes testing, documentation, and training the owner\'s staff. On complex facilities, commissioning catches problems that inspections alone miss, because it tests how systems work together under operating conditions rather than checking individual components.'],
      ['Why do airport projects cost what they cost?', 'Airport construction carries cost drivers that ordinary commercial work does not: security requirements that restrict access and working hours, operational phasing that slows production, specialized systems like airfield lighting and navigational aids, stringent specifications for pavements and safety areas, and extensive documentation and compliance overhead. Comparing an airport project to a non-aviation building of similar size will usually produce a misleading number.'],
      ['How long does a typical airport project take?', 'There is no typical duration — a pavement rehabilitation might take one construction season while a terminal program spans a decade. What matters more than the total is understanding the phases: planning and environmental work often take longer than construction itself on public projects, and procurement adds months that private-sector comparisons tend to omit. Anyone evaluating a timeline should ask which phases are included in the dates quoted.'],
      ['Where do I find official information about a specific airport\'s projects?', 'Start with the airport\'s own website — look for capital improvement programs, board meeting materials, and procurement portals. State aviation agencies often publish grant and project lists. The FAA publishes grant awards and airport data. For federally funded work, procurement notices appear on the relevant public bidding platforms. Meeting minutes from the airport authority or governing board are frequently the most candid source.'],
      ['Can I suggest a topic for this knowledge base?', 'Yes. Use the contact page to send topic suggestions, corrections, or questions. We prioritize topics that help readers evaluate real projects — planning, delivery, funding, and verification — and we welcome pointers to primary sources we may have missed.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights': {
    title: 'Aviation Facilities Insights | Pondco Online',
    description: 'Original educational notes about aviation-facility planning and delivery.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Useful notes for evaluating aviation-facility work.',
    intro: 'Our editorial standard is simple: explain a topic plainly, identify uncertainty, and avoid presenting assumptions or illustrations as verified project facts.',
    sections: [
      ['A good brief reduces rework', 'A strong project brief makes the operating need, users, constraints, decision authority, and success criteria visible. It gives every later decision a shared point of reference.'],
      ['Data needs context', 'Dashboards and milestones can be helpful, but only when their source, owner, date, and limitations are clear. A polished interface does not validate the underlying data.'],
      ['Public trust is built in the details', 'Clear language, accessible documents, accurate status labels, and a way to reach the publisher help readers assess information responsibly.'],
    ],
  
    articles: [
      ['/insights/how-to-read-airport-master-plan', 'How to Read an Airport Master Plan as a Non-Engineer'], ['/insights/writing-defensible-project-scope', 'Writing a Defensible Scope for an Aviation Construction Project'], ['/insights/delivery-methods-compared', 'Design-Bid-Build vs. Design-Build vs. CMAR for Airport Projects'], ['/insights/how-aip-funding-works', 'Airport Grants: How AIP Funding Actually Works'], ['/insights/phasing-construction-active-airfield', 'Phasing Construction on an Active Airfield'], ['/insights/submittals-and-rfis-explained', 'Submittals and RFIs, Explained for Project Newcomers'], ['/insights/commissioning-terminal-systems-checklist', 'Commissioning Terminal Building Systems: A Practical Checklist'], ['/insights/pavement-condition-index-basics', 'Pavement Condition Basics: Understanding PCI for Airfield Pavements'], ['/insights/stakeholder-coordination-airport-projects', 'Who Sits at the Table: Stakeholder Coordination on Public Airport Projects'], ['/insights/project-closeout-done-right', 'Closeout Done Right: Punch Lists, Manuals, Warranties, and Records'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/how-to-read-airport-master-plan': {
    title: 'How to Read an Airport Master Plan as a Non-Engineer | Pondco Online',
    description: 'A plain-language guide to airport master plans: what each chapter does, which numbers matter, and what the document does not promise.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'How to Read an Airport Master Plan as a Non-Engineer',
    intro: 'Airport master plans look intimidating — hundreds of pages of forecasts, drawings, and cost tables. But their structure is predictable, and once you know what each part is for, you can extract what you need without readi',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Airport master plans look intimidating — hundreds of pages of forecasts, drawings, and cost tables. But their structure is predictable, and once you know what each part is for, you can extract what you need without reading every page. This guide walks through a typical plan in the order a curious reader should approach it.'],
      ['Start with the executive summary and the development concept', 'The executive summary tells you what the planners concluded: what the airport needs, roughly when, and at what order of cost. The development concept — usually a drawing of the airport as it could look in twenty years — shows the physical recommendations: new or extended runways, terminal expansions, relocated facilities, land acquisition. Read these first because everything else in the document exists to justify them. If the summary says the terminal needs expansion by 2032, the rest of the plan should show the passenger forecast, the facility analysis, and the cost estimate behind that claim. If you cannot trace a recommendation back to its evidence, that is worth noting.'],
      ['Check the inventory before the forecasts', 'The inventory chapter documents what exists today: runways, taxiways, aprons, buildings, utilities, land use, and environmental conditions. It is the factual baseline. Skim it for the facility you care about and note its stated condition and capacity — this is the "before" picture the plan measures against.'],
      ['Interrogate the forecast', 'The aviation activity forecast projects passengers, aircraft operations, and based aircraft over the planning horizon. It drives nearly every facility recommendation, so it deserves scrutiny. Look for: The assumptions. Forecasts rest on assumptions about the local economy, airline service, and demographics. These should be stated explicitly. A forecast that assumes an airline adds three daily departures is making a bet, not a measurement. The scenarios. Good plans show a range — low, baseline, high — rather than a single line. If the recommended development only makes sense under the high scenario, that tells you something about the risk. The track record. Compare the last plan\'s forecast with what actually happened. Planners sometimes include this; if they do not, you can often find the old plan online and do it yourself. Forecasts are the most uncertain part of any master plan. Treat them as structured guesses, not predictions.'],
      ['Understand facility requirements', 'The facility requirements chapter translates the forecast into physical needs: how much runway length the forecast fleet mix requires, how many gates the peak-hour passengers need, how much apron space the based aircraft demand. This is where engineering standards enter — runway design codes, terminal space planning factors, FAA design standards. You do not need to verify the math. What you need is the logic chain: forecast leads to demand, demand plus standards leads to facility need, facility need leads to the recommended project. If any link is missing or hand-waved, the recommendation is weaker than it looks.'],
      ['Read the alternatives analysis for what was rejected', 'Plans typically evaluate several development alternatives before recommending one. The rejected alternatives are often more informative than the winner: they show what the planners considered and why they set it aside. Common reasons include cost, environmental impact, operational disruption, and land constraints. If your concern is "why didn\'t they consider X," the alternatives chapter is where the answer usually lives.'],
      ['Treat the financial plan as a sketch, not a budget', 'The financial chapter estimates capital costs and identifies potential funding sources — federal grants, state grants, passenger facility charges, airport revenue, bonds. These estimates are planning-level: useful for comparing alternatives and testing affordability, but not a construction budget. Costs at this stage commonly carry wide contingencies, and the funding mix is aspirational until grants are actually awarded.'],
      ['Know what the plan does not do', 'A master plan does not authorize construction, appropriate money, or complete environmental review. It does not bind future boards or guarantee that any recommended project will be built. Many recommendations sit for a decade or more before conditions catch up with them — or never happen at all. When someone cites a master plan as proof that a project "is happening," check whether the project has actually moved into design and funding. The plan is the starting line, not the finish.'],
      ['A thirty-minute reading routine', 'If you have half an hour: read the executive summary, look at the development concept drawing, skim the forecast assumptions, and read the financial chapter\'s funding table. Then pick the one facility you care about and trace it through inventory, requirements, and the recommended project list. You will know more about the airport\'s plans than most people in the room — and you will know exactly which claims are solid and which are still just plans. Keep the plan on file, too: when the next update appears in five or ten years, comparing the two documents is the fastest way to see what the airport actually did versus what it once intended.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/writing-defensible-project-scope': {
    title: 'Writing a Defensible Scope for an Aviation Construction Project | Pondco Online',
    description: 'What makes a construction scope clear, comparable, and fair — and the omissions that generate change orders and disputes.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Writing a Defensible Scope for an Aviation Construction Project',
    intro: 'Most construction disputes are scope disputes wearing different clothes. A change order argument, a bid protest, a schedule claim — trace it back far enough and you usually find a scope that was vague, incomplete, or int',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Most construction disputes are scope disputes wearing different clothes. A change order argument, a bid protest, a schedule claim — trace it back far enough and you usually find a scope that was vague, incomplete, or internally inconsistent. Writing a clear scope is unglamorous work, and it is the highest-return effort in the early project.'],
      ['What a scope must contain', 'A defensible scope answers five questions before a contractor ever sees it: What is the operating problem? State the need in functional terms: the pavement section has reached the end of its service life, the checkpoint cannot process the design-hour passenger load, the lighting system no longer meets standards. The problem statement anchors every later decision. What is the desired outcome? Describe the finished state in measurable terms where possible: reconstructed pavement meeting the specified design life, a checkpoint sized for a stated throughput, a lighting system compliant with the current advisory circular. What is included — and excluded? Boundaries are where disputes breed. If the runway reconstruction includes the blast pads but not the taxiway connectors, say so. If the terminal renovation covers the holdroom but not the concessions build-out, say so. Explicit exclusions are as important as inclusions. Which standards apply? Name the FAA advisory circulars, building codes, and owner standards that govern the work, with versions or dates. "Per applicable standards" is not a standard. What are the constraints? Working hours, phasing requirements, operational restrictions, environmental conditions, funding rules, and milestone dates all shape what the contractor can do and what it will cost.'],
      ['Write for comparability', 'Public owners award to the responsive, responsible bidder — often the lowest. That only works if every bidder prices the same thing. Ambiguity destroys comparability: one bidder assumes the difficult interpretation and prices high, another assumes the easy one and prices low, and the owner ends up with the optimistic bidder and a change order pipeline. Test your scope for comparability by imagining three competent contractors reading it independently. Would they all include the same work? Would they all exclude the same work? Where you hesitate, the scope needs another sentence.'],
      ['Common omissions on aviation projects', 'Certain items are forgotten often enough to deserve a checklist: Phasing and operational constraints. If the work must be sequenced around operations, the phasing plan — or at least the phasing requirements — belongs in the scope, not discovered after award. Testing and inspection responsibilities. Who performs acceptance testing? Who pays for failed tests and retests? Pavement and lighting projects live or die on this language. Submittal and closeout deliverables. Record drawings, O&M manuals, warranties, spare parts, and training should be priced in the bid, not negotiated at the end when the owner\'s leverage is gone. Existing conditions. Differing site conditions are the classic claim. The scope should state what investigations were done, share the reports, and define the process when reality differs. Coordination with others. Airline tenants, FAA technical operations, utility companies, adjacent contractors — name the interfaces and assign responsibility for each.'],
      ['The change-order test', 'Before issuing the scope, run it through the change-order test: for each major element, ask "if the contractor asked for more money for this, could I point to the sentence that says it is included?" If the answer is a shrug, add the sentence. This is not about being adversarial; it is about giving both sides a shared reference so that legitimate changes — and there will be some — can be distinguished from work that was always in the contract.'],
      ['A miniature example', 'Consider a taxiway lighting rehabilitation. A weak scope says: "Rehabilitate taxiway lighting on Taxiway B." A defensible scope says: "Remove and replace the taxiway edge lighting system on Taxiway B between Taxiway A and the Runway 9 hold-short line, including regulators, cable in conduit, fixtures, and signage; provide temporary lighting maintaining the taxiway operational each night per the phasing plan; perform acceptance testing per the specifications; deliver record drawings and O&M manuals. Excludes the apron floodlighting and the runway guard lights, which remain in service." The second version can be priced, scheduled, inspected, and closed out. The first version can only be argued about. Notice what the strong version does not do: it does not describe means and methods, which are the contractor\'s domain, and it does not narrate the design rationale. It states the work, the boundaries, the standards, and the deliverables — the four things a bidder needs and an inspector will check.'],
      ['Reviewing someone else\'s scope', 'If you inherit a scope rather than writing one — as a reviewer, a bidder, or a new team member — read it adversarially. Mark every sentence that could mean two things. List every interface with another contract or another party and check that responsibility is assigned. Confirm that the phasing, testing, and closeout requirements are present, not implied. Then send the questions back before pricing or approval, because every ambiguity you accept now becomes a negotiation later, on worse terms.'],
      ['Keep it current', 'Scopes decay. Between planning and procurement, conditions change: a tenant\'s needs shift, a funding source imposes new requirements, a design refinement moves a boundary. Before the solicitation goes out, walk the scope against the current design and the current constraints one more time. The hour this takes is cheap insurance against the most preventable kind of dispute.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/delivery-methods-compared': {
    title: 'Design-Bid-Build vs. Design-Build vs. CMAR for Airport Projects | Pondco Online',
    description: 'How the three common delivery methods distribute risk, control, and speed on aviation construction — and how to choose between them.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Design-Bid-Build vs. Design-Build vs. CMAR for Airport Projects',
    intro: 'The delivery method decides who contracts with whom, who carries which risks, and when the builder\'s knowledge enters the project. On airport work — where operations constrain construction and the systems are specialized',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'The delivery method decides who contracts with whom, who carries which risks, and when the builder\'s knowledge enters the project. On airport work — where operations constrain construction and the systems are specialized — the choice matters more than it does for ordinary buildings. Here is how the three common methods compare.'],
      ['Design-bid-build: the traditional sequence', 'The owner contracts separately with a designer and a builder. Design is completed (or nearly so), then contractors bid on the finished documents, and the lowest responsive, responsible bidder builds it. Strengths. The owner keeps full control of the design and gets genuine price competition on a defined scope. The roles are clear, the process is familiar to every public agency, and the procurement record is straightforward to defend. Weaknesses. The builder\'s practical knowledge arrives too late to improve the design. Design errors become the owner\'s problem to fix through change orders. The schedule is strictly sequential — construction cannot meaningfully start until design is done — which makes this the slowest method. Adversarial relationships are common because the builder did not participate in the decisions it must now execute. Best fit. Well-defined projects with complete designs, minimal unknowns, and no schedule emergency: a straightforward pavement rehabilitation, a standard building renovation, work where the owner has strong in-house technical capacity to manage the designer-builder interface.'],
      ['Design-build: one contract for design and construction', 'The owner contracts with a single design-build entity — usually a contractor teamed with designers — that is responsible for both designing and building the project to performance requirements the owner defines. Strengths. Schedule compresses because design and construction overlap; the builder can start early packages while later design is still being finished. There is a single point of responsibility — no designer-builder finger-pointing. The builder\'s means, methods, and supply-chain knowledge shape the design from the start, which tends to produce more buildable documents. Weaknesses. The owner gives up direct control of design details and must instead define performance requirements precisely — which is harder than it sounds. Price competition happens at the proposal stage on an incomplete design, so comparing proposals requires sophisticated evaluation. Changes after award are expensive because the owner is negotiating with a single entity that holds all the cards. The owner\'s team needs the expertise to evaluate technical proposals, not just low bids. Best fit. Schedule-driven projects, projects where construction innovation matters (complex phasing on an active airfield, specialized systems), and owners who can write strong performance criteria and evaluate technical proposals rigorously.'],
      ['Construction manager at risk: early builder involvement, owner-held design contracts', 'The owner hires the designer directly and also hires a construction manager during design. The CM advises on cost, schedule, phasing, and constructability as the design develops, then commits to a guaranteed maximum price and builds the project, usually through subcontractor bidding. Strengths. The owner keeps the designer relationship and design control while gaining the builder\'s input during design — the best of both worlds in theory. The GMP provides cost certainty at a defined point. Subcontractor work is competitively bid, preserving price tension below the CM. Phasing and operational constraints — the hard part of airport work — get professional attention before construction starts. Weaknesses. The GMP negotiation is the moment of maximum tension: set it too early and the price carries heavy contingency; set it too late and the schedule advantage evaporates. The owner pays a CM fee on top of design and construction costs, so it is the most expensive method to administer. If the CM relationship sours, the owner has limited leverage mid-project. Success depends heavily on the quality of the CM and the owner\'s ability to manage a collaborative-but-accountable relationship. Best fit. Complex, high-risk projects where early contractor input is valuable but the owner wants to retain design control: terminal programs, projects with brutal phasing constraints, work with significant unknowns that need managing during design rather than after bidding.'],
      ['How owners actually choose', 'The honest selection criteria are risk, control, schedule, and capacity: Where is the biggest risk? Unknown subsurface conditions argue for early builder involvement. A tight operational window argues for schedule compression. Political sensitivity argues for maximum price competition and a defensible record. How much control does the owner need? Owners with strong design standards and tenant coordination needs often prefer to keep the designer direct. What can the owner\'s team manage? Design-build demands proposal evaluation skill; CMAR demands active management of a three-party relationship; design-bid-build demands the capacity to resolve designer-builder conflicts. What does the funding allow? Some grant programs and procurement codes restrict or prescribe delivery methods. Check before falling in love with an approach. There is no universally best method — only the method whose tradeoffs match the project\'s actual risks. The owners who choose well are the ones who name their biggest risk first and pick the method that puts the most capable party on that risk.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/how-aip-funding-works': {
    title: 'Airport Grants: How AIP Funding Actually Works | Pondco Online',
    description: 'The Airport Improvement Program explained: where the money comes from, what it can pay for, and the obligations that come with it.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Airport Grants: How AIP Funding Actually Works',
    intro: 'The Airport Improvement Program is the federal government\'s primary grant mechanism for airport development. It distributes billions annually, but the money comes with a rulebook that shapes every project it touches. Und',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'The Airport Improvement Program is the federal government\'s primary grant mechanism for airport development. It distributes billions annually, but the money comes with a rulebook that shapes every project it touches. Understanding that rulebook is essential for anyone evaluating a federally funded airport project.'],
      ['Where the money comes from', 'AIP is funded through the Airport and Airway Trust Fund, which collects revenue from taxes on airline tickets, cargo waybills, and aviation fuel. Congress authorizes the program\'s spending levels, and the FAA distributes the money as grants to eligible airport sponsors — typically the public agency that owns and operates the airport. This matters for two reasons. First, AIP money is not general tax revenue; it is user-derived, which is why it is restricted to aviation purposes. Second, the amount available in any year depends on congressional action, so funding levels and program rules can shift with each reauthorization.'],
      ['What the money can pay for', 'AIP funds planning and development that serves the airport\'s aeronautical purpose: runways, taxiways, aprons, lighting, navigational aids, safety areas, terminal buildings (the public, non-revenue portions), noise compatibility, and airport planning studies including master plans. The key test is generally whether the project is shown on the FAA-approved airport layout plan and serves eligible airport purposes. What it typically cannot pay for: revenue-producing facilities like parking garages and rental car facilities, purely commercial development, and operating expenses. Terminal projects get particular scrutiny — the ticket counters and holdrooms may be eligible while the food court is not. The eligibility determination is project-specific, and sponsors confirm it with the FAA before counting on the money.'],
      ['Entitlements vs. discretionary grants', 'AIP money reaches airports through two channels. Entitlement funds are distributed by formula based on passenger enplanements and cargo volume — the airport earns them by existing and handling traffic, and can generally count on them year to year. Discretionary funds are awarded competitively for high-priority projects, with the FAA weighing factors like the project\'s role in the national airport system and the sponsor\'s needs. In practice, most airport capital programs blend both: entitlements provide the predictable base, discretionary grants fund the big-ticket items, and the sponsor fills the gaps with state grants, passenger facility charges, and airport revenue. The funding mix for any given project is worth understanding because each source has its own timing and rules.'],
      ['The grant assurances: obligations that outlast the project', 'Accepting AIP money means accepting the grant assurances — a set of obligations the sponsor agrees to as a condition of the grant. Key ones include keeping the airport available for public aeronautical use, not discriminating among aeronautical users, maintaining the facilities built with federal money, and using airport revenue for airport purposes. These obligations run with the airport, not just the project. An assurance accepted for a runway grant in 2010 still constrains what the sponsor can do with airport land today. Anyone evaluating an airport\'s plans — a land-use proposal, a rate dispute, a closure question — needs to know which assurances are in force, because they often decide the issue.'],
      ['Procurement and compliance requirements', 'Federally funded procurement must follow federal requirements layered on top of state and local rules: competitive procurement, Buy American provisions for steel and manufactured products, disadvantaged business enterprise goals, prevailing wage requirements on construction, and specific contract provisions the FAA requires in the documents. These add real cost and real schedule — the compliance overhead on an AIP project is one reason airport construction costs what it does.'],
      ['The fiscal calendar and project timing', 'Federal money moves on the federal fiscal year, and that shapes project schedules in concrete ways. Grant offers typically arrive mid-year, funds must be obligated within statutory windows, and a project that misses its grant cycle may wait a full year for the next one. Sponsors plan their design and procurement milestones backward from these dates: the bid opening has to land so that award, grant execution, and notice to proceed all fit inside the funding window. This is also why multi-year programs get built in annual pieces. An entitlement accrues each year, a discretionary grant covers one project phase, and the sponsor sequences design packages so that each year\'s money has a shovel-ready project waiting for it. When an airport\'s capital plan shows the same project split across three fiscal years, it is usually the funding calendar — not the construction logic — doing the splitting. Readers evaluating a timeline should always ask which parts are driven by the work and which by the money.'],
      ['The practical takeaways', 'For readers evaluating a project: check whether it is AIP-funded and which portions are eligible, because that determines both the money and the rules. Expect the funding to arrive in phases across fiscal years, which is why multi-year projects get built in pieces. And remember that the grant assurances shape the airport\'s obligations long after the ribbon-cutting — the fine print of a grant accepted decades ago may be the most important document in a current dispute.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/phasing-construction-active-airfield': {
    title: 'Phasing Construction on an Active Airfield | Pondco Online',
    description: 'How airport projects sequence work around live operations: phasing plans, safety areas, night work, and communication with tenants.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Phasing Construction on an Active Airfield',
    intro: 'Rebuilding a runway while airplanes keep using the airport is one of the harder problems in construction. The work has to be divided into segments that can be built safely while the rest of the facility operates — and ev',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Rebuilding a runway while airplanes keep using the airport is one of the harder problems in construction. The work has to be divided into segments that can be built safely while the rest of the facility operates — and every segment needs its own safety plan, access routes, and communication. This is what a phasing plan does, and on aviation projects it is often as important as the design itself.'],
      ['Why phasing dominates airport construction', 'On a normal site, the contractor organizes work for efficiency: the best sequence, the fewest mobilizations, the fastest path. On an airfield, operations organize the work instead. Aircraft need safe, predictable surfaces and clear approaches at all times. That means construction areas must be physically separated from movement areas, haul routes cannot cross active taxiways without controls, and temporary conditions — lighting, signage, markings — must meet standards every single day, not just at the end. The practical effect is that the schedule is built around operational windows rather than construction logic. A runway might be reconstructed in halves or thirds, with each segment closed, built, and reopened before the next begins. Night work becomes common because flight activity is lightest. None of this is efficient in the conventional sense, and all of it costs money — which is why airfield work prices higher than equivalent off-airport construction.'],
      ['What a phasing plan contains', 'A serious phasing plan is a document, not a sketch. It typically includes: Phase limits and sequence. Exactly which areas are under construction in each phase, in what order, and what triggers the move from one phase to the next. Operational configuration per phase. Which runways, taxiways, and aprons remain open, and under what restrictions — shortened lengths, displaced thresholds, weight limits. Construction access and haul routes. How workers, equipment, and materials get to the work area without entering movement areas, including gate locations and escort requirements. Safety areas and separations. Distances between construction activity and operating surfaces, barricade and marking requirements, and how they change between phases. Temporary lighting, signage, and markings. What gets installed each night or each phase change to keep the airfield compliant — closed runway markings, temporary edge lights, construction signage. Communications plan. Who notifies whom about phase changes: airport operations, air traffic control, airlines, tenants, and through notices to air missions for the broader aviation community.'],
      ['Safety management during phased work', 'The FAA expects construction safety to be managed explicitly, and the Construction Safety and Phasing Plan is the standard vehicle. It identifies the hazards each phase creates — equipment near movement areas, stockpiles affecting line of sight, temporary grade changes — and the controls for each. The plan is typically reviewed by airport operations and the FAA before work begins, and it is a living document: when conditions change, the plan changes. Two principles recur. First, positive separation beats procedures: a physical barrier or a closed taxiway is more reliable than a rule everyone must remember. Second, the nighttime configuration matters as much as the daytime one — an airfield that is safe at noon can be hazardous at midnight if the temporary lighting and markings are not right.'],
      ['Coordinating with tenants and operations', 'Airlines, cargo operators, fixed-base operators, and other tenants all have operations that phasing disrupts. Early coordination — before the phasing plan is final — surfaces constraints the designers might not know: a cargo carrier\'s peak season, an airline\'s schedule change, a tenant\'s fuel delivery route. Late-discovered constraints cause the expensive kind of rework, where a phase has to be redesigned after bidding. Airport operations staff are the critical partner throughout. They own the airfield every day, they know its rhythms, and they will be the ones implementing the daily transitions between construction and operational configurations. Projects that treat operations as a reviewer at the end get a worse plan than projects that treat operations as a co-author from the start.'],
      ['The cost of phasing', 'Phasing costs money in ways that are easy to underestimate: mobilization and demobilization for each phase, temporary facilities installed and removed repeatedly, reduced crew productivity from constrained access and night work, extended general conditions from a longer schedule, and the management overhead of coordinating it all. When comparing a phased approach to alternatives — or when evaluating why a bid came in high — these costs should be visible and explicit rather than buried in a lump sum.'],
      ['Reading a phasing plan as an outsider', 'If you are reviewing a project — as a board member, journalist, or concerned citizen — ask for the phasing plan and read it with three questions: What stays open during each phase, and is that realistic? What could go wrong between phases, and what is the contingency? Who was consulted, and were the tenants\' operational constraints actually accommodated? The answers reveal whether the project team has thought seriously about the hardest part of airport construction: building it while the airport keeps working.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/submittals-and-rfis-explained': {
    title: 'Submittals and RFIs, Explained for Project Newcomers | Pondco Online',
    description: 'The two paperwork engines of construction administration: what submittals and RFIs are, how the review cycle works, and where projects get stuck.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Submittals and RFIs, Explained for Project Newcomers',
    intro: 'Two document flows carry most of the technical communication on a construction project: submittals, where the contractor proposes what to install, and RFIs — requests for information — where the contractor asks the desig',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Two document flows carry most of the technical communication on a construction project: submittals, where the contractor proposes what to install, and RFIs — requests for information — where the contractor asks the designer to clarify the documents. Understanding how they work explains a surprising amount of how projects actually get built.'],
      ['Submittals: proving the work matches the design', 'The specifications describe materials and equipment by performance and standard: a particular pavement mix design, a specified light fixture type, a pump with defined capacity. But the contractor does not simply buy them — first, the contractor submits product data, shop drawings, and samples demonstrating that the proposed items meet the specification, and the design team reviews them before anything is ordered or installed. The submittal register, usually prepared from the specifications, lists every required submittal: product data sheets, shop drawings, material certifications, test reports, warranties, and closeout documents. On a large aviation project this can run to hundreds of items. Each one follows a cycle: contractor prepares, designer reviews, and the submittal comes back marked approved, approved as noted, revise and resubmit, or rejected. The review clock matters. Contracts typically give the designer a set number of days to review each submittal, and late reviews can delay procurement of long-lead items — airfield lighting equipment, baggage systems, and custom electrical gear are classic examples. Savvy contractors submit early and track the log relentlessly; projects get stuck when submittals pile up unreviewed or when a rejected submittal restarts a six-week procurement cycle.'],
      ['What reviewers are actually checking', 'Design review of submittals is not a rubber stamp and not a redesign. The reviewer checks conformance with the design intent: does this fixture meet the specified photometrics, does this mix design meet the strength and durability requirements, does this shop drawing coordinate with the adjacent work. The contractor remains responsible for means, methods, and quantities — the review confirms the proposal matches the documents, nothing more. This division of responsibility is why the review stamps carry careful language. "Approved" on a submittal does not transfer design liability to the reviewer, and contractors who treat it as a blanket endorsement learn this the expensive way when a non-conforming installation has to come out.'],
      ['RFIs: resolving what the documents do not say', 'No set of construction documents is perfect. Drawings conflict, details are missing, existing conditions differ from what was shown, and specifications reference standards ambiguously. The RFI is the formal mechanism for resolving these gaps: the contractor describes the problem, proposes a solution or asks for direction, and the designer responds with a clarification. A healthy project generates RFIs steadily — they are evidence that someone is reading the documents carefully before building, not a sign of failure. What matters is the response time and the quality of the answers. RFIs that sit unanswered become schedule delays; vague answers generate follow-up RFIs; and a pattern of RFIs pointing at the same drawing area usually signals a design coordination problem that needs direct attention rather than another round of paperwork.'],
      ['Where the two flows intersect — and where projects stall', 'Submittals and RFIs interact constantly. A submittal may reveal that the specified product is discontinued, generating an RFI about an acceptable substitution. An RFI answer may change a detail, requiring revised shop drawings. Managing the two logs together — rather than as separate administrative chores — is what keeps the technical side of a project moving. Common stall points are predictable: submittals sent late and then blamed on the reviewer, RFIs used as delay claims rather than genuine questions, designers who answer RFIs verbally without documenting the response, and substitution requests dressed up as "or equal" proposals that are not actually equal. Each has a contractual answer, but the practical fix is the same: disciplined logs, stated review times, written responses, and a weekly review of what is stuck and why.'],
      ['A final habit', 'The teams that handle submittals and RFIs well share one habit: they review the logs together every week and ask what is stuck, who owns it, and what unblocks it. Fifteen minutes of disciplined review prevents the slow accumulation of overdue items that quietly becomes a month of delay. The paperwork is not the project — but on a well-run job, the paperwork tells you exactly where the project stands.'],
      ['Reading the logs as an outsider', 'If you ever get access to a project\'s submittal and RFI logs — through a public records request, for example — they are remarkably revealing. The RFI log shows where the design was weakest. The submittal log shows whether procurement stayed ahead of construction. The aging of open items shows whether the team was managing the work or being managed by it. For anyone trying to understand why a project went well or badly, these unglamorous logs are often the best evidence available.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/commissioning-terminal-systems-checklist': {
    title: 'Commissioning Terminal Building Systems: A Practical Checklist | Pondco Online',
    description: 'What commissioning covers in an airport terminal — HVAC, electrical, life safety, baggage, and controls — and how to structure it so systems work together.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Commissioning Terminal Building Systems: A Practical Checklist',
    intro: 'Inspections confirm that components were installed correctly. Commissioning confirms that systems work — and work together — under operating conditions. On an airport terminal, where HVAC, electrical, life safety, baggag',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Inspections confirm that components were installed correctly. Commissioning confirms that systems work — and work together — under operating conditions. On an airport terminal, where HVAC, electrical, life safety, baggage handling, and controls all interact, commissioning is the difference between a building that passed inspection and a building that actually performs. Here is how to think about it.'],
      ['Start with the owner\'s requirements', 'Every commissioning effort should begin with a written statement of what the owner expects the systems to do: temperature and humidity ranges in the holdrooms, lighting levels on the apron-facing curtain wall at night, baggage throughput at peak hour, recovery time after a power transfer. Without this document, commissioning has no pass/fail criteria — it becomes a vague exercise in "checking things." The owner\'s requirements should be written before design is complete, because they are what the design is supposed to satisfy. On public projects they also become the reference the commissioning agent tests against, so precision pays: "comfortable" is not a requirement, but a temperature range with an occupancy schedule is.'],
      ['Scope the systems explicitly', 'A terminal commissioning scope typically covers: HVAC. Air handling units, chillers, boilers, terminal units, exhaust systems, and the building automation system that controls them. Functional testing verifies sequences of operation: economizer modes, morning warm-up, occupied/unoccupied scheduling, and failure responses. Electrical. Normal and emergency power distribution, generators, automatic transfer switches, UPS systems for critical loads, and lighting controls. Testing includes load-bank testing of generators and witnessed transfers. Life safety. Fire alarm, sprinkler, smoke control, and emergency communication systems — tested in coordination with the fire marshal\'s acceptance testing, not as a substitute for it. Plumbing and specialty systems. Domestic water, drainage, and any specialty systems such as aircraft rescue vehicle bays or fueling support areas. Baggage handling. Throughput testing under simulated peak loads, jam detection and clearing, sortation accuracy, and integration with the airline systems. Building automation and integration. The layer that ties everything together — and the most common source of commissioning findings, because individual systems pass their own tests while the integrated sequences fail.'],
      ['Structure the process in phases', 'Commissioning is not a single event at the end. It runs through the project: Design review. The commissioning agent reviews the design for commissionability: Are there test ports? Can the systems be isolated for testing? Are the sequences of operation written clearly enough to test against? Submittal review. The agent checks that submitted equipment can actually perform the specified sequences — catching mismatches before procurement. Installation verification. Checklists confirm that equipment is installed per the documents and ready for startup: rotation checks, control point verification, sensor calibration. Functional testing. The core of the work — running systems through their sequences under real or simulated conditions and documenting the results. Training and documentation. Operations staff are trained on the systems as commissioned, and the systems manual documents how everything is supposed to work. Warranty-period review. A return visit near the end of the warranty period catches seasonal issues — the cooling problem that only appears in August, the heating sequence that only fails in January.'],
      ['Common findings and what they reveal', 'Commissioning reports from terminal projects show recurring patterns: sequences of operation that were never fully written and therefore never fully implemented, sensors installed in locations where they cannot read representative conditions, systems from different vendors whose integration was assumed rather than specified, and operating staff who were never trained on the building they inherited. Each finding is cheap to fix during construction and expensive to live with for twenty years.'],
      ['The systems manual', 'The deliverable that ties commissioning together is the systems manual: a single document describing how the facility\'s systems are intended to operate, the sequences they run, the setpoints they hold, and how they interact. Unlike the O&M manuals, which are equipment-focused, the systems manual is operations-focused — it answers "how do I run this building" rather than "how do I service this unit." Owners should require it explicitly, because it is the document the night-shift operator actually needs at 2 a.m.'],
      ['Budget and schedule it honestly', 'A realistic commissioning budget for a complex terminal is a meaningful line item, not a rounding error — and the schedule needs testing windows that do not get compressed when construction runs late. The most common failure mode is not bad commissioning but evaporated commissioning: the testing window gets squeezed by a delayed substantial completion, the functional tests get abbreviated, and the building opens with systems nobody has seen work together. Protect the commissioning schedule the way you protect any other critical path — because it is one. And insist on witnessing the tests that matter most to operations: the power transfer, the baggage peak-load run, the smoke control sequence. A witnessed test that passes is worth a file of untested checklists.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/pavement-condition-index-basics': {
    title: 'Pavement Condition Basics: Understanding PCI for Airfield Pavements | Pondco Online',
    description: 'What the Pavement Condition Index measures, how airfield pavement surveys work, and how PCI numbers should — and should not — guide decisions.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Pavement Condition Basics: Understanding PCI for Airfield Pavements',
    intro: 'When airport staff say a runway "has a PCI of 62," they are quoting the Pavement Condition Index — the standard 0-to-100 score for pavement surface condition. PCI numbers drive maintenance budgets and rehabilitation timi',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'When airport staff say a runway "has a PCI of 62," they are quoting the Pavement Condition Index — the standard 0-to-100 score for pavement surface condition. PCI numbers drive maintenance budgets and rehabilitation timing across the industry. But a PCI is a specific measurement with specific limits, and decisions go wrong when the number is treated as more than it is.'],
      ['What PCI actually measures', 'PCI is a visual survey score. Trained inspectors walk or drive the pavement, identify distresses — cracking, rutting, raveling, joint spalling, patching, and others — and record each distress\'s type, severity, and extent within defined sample areas. The observations feed a standardized calculation that deducts points from 100 based on what was found. The result is a snapshot of surface condition at the time of the survey. Two things follow from this definition. First, PCI measures what the eye can see: surface distress, not structural capacity. A pavement can look acceptable while its base is failing, and it can look rough while remaining structurally sound. Second, PCI is a point in time. Pavements deteriorate, and the rate depends on traffic, climate, drainage, and maintenance — so a three-year-old PCI is history, not data.'],
      ['How airfield surveys differ from street surveys', 'The method is the same family used for roads, but airfields apply it with their own standards and stakes. Sample areas are laid out on runways, taxiways, and aprons; safety protocols govern who can be on the movement areas and when; and the distress catalog includes airfield-specific concerns. The FAA\'s guidance standardizes the approach so that PCI numbers are comparable across airports — which is exactly what makes them useful for grant prioritization and network-level planning. Survey frequency is typically on a multi-year cycle for the full network, with more frequent looks at pavements known to be deteriorating. Between surveys, operations staff and maintenance crews provide the informal monitoring that catches sudden changes — the new crack that appeared after a hard winter, the settling near a drainage structure.'],
      ['Reading a PCI number', 'The scale runs 0 (failed) to 100 (excellent), with conventional bands: scores in the 85–100 range are generally good, 70–85 satisfactory, 55–70 fair, 40–55 poor, and below that various degrees of failed. But the band boundaries are conventions, not physics. The useful reading of a PCI is comparative and trend-based: Compare within the network. Which pavements are deteriorating fastest? Where does the next dollar of maintenance buy the most condition? Watch the trend. A pavement dropping from 78 to 71 over one cycle is telling you something different than one stable at 68 for six years. Pair it with age and traffic. A ten-year-old taxiway at PCI 70 and a two-year-old runway at PCI 70 are different situations demanding different responses.'],
      ['What PCI cannot tell you', 'PCI does not measure structural capacity — that requires deflection testing or coring. It does not measure friction or smoothness directly, though severe distress correlates with both. It does not account for what is underneath: drainage conditions, base quality, and subgrade strength all affect how long a rehabilitation will last, and none of them appear in the score. And it does not set priorities by itself — a low-PCI service road and a medium-PCI primary runway do not deserve equal attention, because their operational consequences differ enormously.'],
      ['Getting a survey done', 'A proper PCI survey is a contracted professional service, not a walkaround. The surveyor lays out sample units across the pavement network, identifies and measures each distress by type and severity, and calculates the index per the standardized method. Airport sponsors typically procure these surveys on a multi-year cycle through their pavement management program, and the results feed directly into the capital planning process. If you are on the owner side, the useful specification is not just "perform a PCI survey" but the network definition, the survey interval, the deliverable format, and how the results integrate with the maintenance plan — otherwise you get a number with no program behind it.'],
      ['How PCI should guide decisions', 'Used well, PCI is a planning tool. It feeds pavement management systems that model deterioration, forecast future condition under different funding scenarios, and identify the optimal timing for treatments — the well-known principle that timely preventive maintenance costs far less than deferred reconstruction. It supports grant applications with objective, comparable data. And it gives boards and the public a comprehensible picture of infrastructure condition. Used badly, PCI becomes a target that distorts decisions: chasing a network average while a critical runway deteriorates, or treating a single survey as a verdict rather than one input among several. The number is a good servant and a bad master. The airports that manage pavements best are the ones that combine the PCI data with structural testing, operational judgment, and an honest assessment of what the budget can actually do.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/stakeholder-coordination-airport-projects': {
    title: 'Who Sits at the Table: Stakeholder Coordination on Public Airport Projects | Pondco Online',
    description: 'Mapping the parties on a public airport project — owners, tenants, regulators, funders, communities — and how to coordinate them.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Who Sits at the Table: Stakeholder Coordination on Public Airport Projects',
    intro: 'Ask who the "client" is on a public airport project and you will get several correct answers. The airport owner holds the contracts, the airlines hold the operational leverage, the FAA holds the regulatory authority, and',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Ask who the "client" is on a public airport project and you will get several correct answers. The airport owner holds the contracts, the airlines hold the operational leverage, the FAA holds the regulatory authority, and the public holds the ultimate accountability. Coordinating these parties — each with different power, timelines, and interests — is a discipline of its own.'],
      ['The owner and its governing body', 'The airport sponsor — a city, county, authority, or port — owns the project and bears the legal responsibility. But the sponsor acts through people: the airport director and staff who manage day-to-day, and the board, commission, or council that approves budgets, awards contracts, and sets policy. These two layers move at different speeds. Staff can resolve a technical question in a meeting; the board resolves it at the next scheduled session, which may be a month away. Project schedules that ignore the board calendar discover this the hard way.'],
      ['Airlines and tenants', 'Airlines are not just customers of the airport; on major projects they are effectively co-designers. Their lease agreements — often negotiated as part of a terminal program — give them approval rights over scope, cost, and schedule elements that affect their operation. Even where formal approval rights do not exist, an airline that cannot operate during construction is a problem no phasing plan can solve without its cooperation. Other tenants matter too: cargo operators with their own peak seasons, fixed-base operators serving general aviation, concessionaires whose businesses live or die on passenger flow, rental car companies with facility needs. Each tenant has operational constraints the project must accommodate, and each learns about the project through its own channels — which means the project has to communicate through those channels, not just publish a website.'],
      ['Regulators', 'The FAA\'s roles are multiple and distinct: airspace review of anything affecting flight paths, design standard compliance for airfield geometry, environmental review under federal law, and grant oversight for funded projects. These are separate processes with separate timelines, often handled by different FAA offices. Treating "the FAA" as a single approver is a common planning error. State aviation agencies add funding oversight and sometimes their own design or procurement requirements. Local authorities handle building permits, land use, and utilities. The TSA governs security screening checkpoints and secure areas — a stakeholder that enters late in many terminal projects and finds decisions it wishes had been made differently. Environmental regulators — federal, state, and sometimes tribal — have jurisdiction over wetlands, stormwater, air quality, and historic resources.'],
      ['Funders', 'Whoever pays gets a say. Federal and state grant agencies impose eligibility rules, procurement requirements, and reporting obligations that shape the project from the solicitation onward. Bondholders and rating agencies care about the financial plan\'s credibility. Passenger facility charge programs require airline consultation and FAA approval. Each funding source arrives on its own timeline with its own conditions, and the project schedule is often a negotiation between construction logic and funding availability.'],
      ['The community', 'Airports exist inside communities, and major projects affect them: construction traffic, noise, land acquisition, environmental impacts, and the economic promise of improved facilities. Community engagement is partly a legal requirement — environmental review mandates public participation — and partly a practical necessity. Projects that engage early and honestly tend to move faster than projects that treat the public as an obstacle, because the concerns surface while there is still time to address them. Elected officials sit at the intersection of community and governance. They answer to constituents, approve funding, and can accelerate or stall a project with a single vote. Keeping them informed — with honest status, not just good news — is basic project stewardship.'],
      ['Making coordination work', 'A few practices separate projects that coordinate well from those that do not: Map approvals early. For every major decision, name who approves it, in what sequence, and on what timeline. Put the board meetings, FAA review cycles, and tenant consultations on the master schedule as real activities. Communicate in each party\'s language. The board needs cost and risk; the airlines need operational impacts; the FAA needs compliance documentation; the public needs plain language. One message, many translations. Resolve conflicts at the right level. Technical disagreements belong with technical staff; policy disagreements belong with the governing body. Escalating too fast politicizes routine issues; escalating too slowly lets them fester. Document agreements. Verbal understandings with tenants and regulators evaporate under pressure. Memoranda of understanding, meeting minutes, and written concurrences are the coordination record. The underlying truth is simple: on a public airport project, authority is distributed and no party can succeed alone. Coordination is not overhead — it is the work. The project manager who maps the stakeholders early, communicates in each party\'s language, and documents every agreement will spend less time fighting fires and more time building the project.'],
    ],
  },
  '/insights/project-closeout-done-right': {
    title: 'Closeout Done Right: Punch Lists, Manuals, Warranties, and Records | Pondco Online',
    description: 'How to close out an aviation construction project properly: punch lists, O&M manuals, warranties, training, and record documents.',
    eyebrow: 'Insights',
    heading: 'Closeout Done Right: Punch Lists, Manuals, Warranties, and Records',
    intro: 'Projects are not finished when construction ends. They are finished when the owner can operate, maintain, and account for what was built — which requires a set of deliverables that too many projects treat as an afterthou',
    sections: [
      ['Overview', 'Projects are not finished when construction ends. They are finished when the owner can operate, maintain, and account for what was built — which requires a set of deliverables that too many projects treat as an afterthought. Closeout done right is planned from the beginning and managed as its own phase.'],
      ['The punch list', 'The punch list is the formal record of work that is incomplete, defective, or not yet accepted. It is developed through inspections as the work nears completion, and it becomes the contractor\'s to-do list for the final push. A good punch list is specific: location, description, and the standard the work must meet. "Fix the lighting" is not a punch list item; "replace the flickering high-mast fixture at the northeast apron, per the specified lamp type" is. Two disciplines make punch lists work. First, generate them early enough that the items can actually be completed before the owner\'s operations depend on the facility — discovering forty electrical deficiencies the week before a terminal opens is a crisis, not a list. Second, track each item to verified completion, not to the contractor\'s assertion. Re-inspection is part of the process.'],
      ['Operation and maintenance manuals', 'The O&M manuals are the owner\'s instruction book for the facility: equipment data, operating procedures, maintenance schedules, troubleshooting guidance, and parts lists for every system installed. On an aviation project this spans airfield lighting regulators, baggage systems, HVAC plants, fire protection, security systems, and more. Manuals fail in predictable ways: they arrive as a box of manufacturer cut-sheets with no organization, they describe the equipment as sold rather than as installed, or they never arrive at all because nobody withheld payment against them. The fix is contractual and procedural — specify what the manuals must contain, require draft submittal before substantial completion, and tie final payment to their acceptance. An owner who cannot maintain the facility does not fully own it.'],
      ['Warranties and guarantees', 'Construction warranties typically run one year from substantial completion, with longer periods for specific items like roofing, pavements, or major equipment. Managing warranties means knowing what they cover, when they expire, and how to invoke them. The practical steps: compile a warranty register with dates and contacts, inspect the work before each warranty expires, and document deficiencies in writing within the warranty period. Extended manufacturer warranties deserve attention during procurement, not after — the time to confirm that a twenty-year roof warranty is actually in place is before the roof is bought, verified through the submittal process.'],
      ['Training', 'Systems are only as good as the people who run them. Closeout training should cover normal operations, seasonal changeover, troubleshooting, and emergency procedures for the facility\'s systems — and it should be delivered to the staff who will actually do the work, not just their supervisors. Video-recorded training sessions, where practical, give future staff something to consult when the original trainees move on. Training that happens six months after opening, from memory, is not training.'],
      ['Record documents', 'The record drawings — often called as-builts — show what was actually constructed, incorporating every change made during the work. They are the reference for all future maintenance, renovation, and expansion. Their value compounds over decades: the engineer designing a 2040 terminal expansion will depend on the accuracy of the 2026 record drawings. Accuracy requires process, not good intentions. The contractor should maintain a current marked-up set throughout construction, not reconstruct it from memory at the end. The design team should review the record set before accepting it. And the owner should store it where future teams can find it — a flat file in a drawer is better than nothing, but a managed digital archive is the standard worth meeting.'],
      ['Financial closeout', 'The administrative tail — final change orders, release of retainage, final payment applications, lien releases, and the reconciliation of project accounts — deserves the same discipline as the technical closeout. Retainage is the owner\'s leverage; releasing it before the punch list is complete and the manuals are accepted surrenders that leverage for nothing. Final audits of grant-funded work can come months or years later, which is another reason the documentation must be complete and organized.'],
      ['Planning closeout from day one', 'Every element above is easier when it is in the contract documents from the start: submittal requirements for manuals, training obligations with attendee lists, warranty documentation standards, record-drawing procedures, and payment tied to closeout deliverables. Closeout planned at the beginning is a phase; closeout discovered at the end is a scramble. The difference shows up in the facility\'s first year of operation — and in every year after. Owners who treat closeout as a deliverable, with the same seriousness as concrete or steel, inherit buildings they can actually run. Those who do not inherit mysteries. A disciplined closeout is the final proof that the project was managed, not merely built.'],
    ],
  },
}

function Seo({ title, description, noIndex = false }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = title
    const setMeta = (name, content) => {
      let element = document.querySelector(`meta[name="${name}"]`)
      if (!element) { element = document.createElement('meta'); element.name = name; document.head.append(element) }
      element.content = content
    }
    setMeta('description', description)
    setMeta('robots', noIndex ? 'noindex, nofollow' : 'index, follow')
  }, [title, description, noIndex])
  return null
}

function ContentPage({ page }) {
  return <>
    <Seo title={page.title} description={page.description} />
    <article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-10 py-12 sm:py-20">
      <header className="space-y-5"><p className="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">{page.eyebrow}</p><h1 className="text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-white sm:text-6xl">{page.heading}</h1><p className="max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">{page.intro}</p></header>
      <div className="space-y-8">{page.sections.map(([heading, text]) => <section key={heading} className="rounded-2xl border border-slate-700 bg-slate-900/70 p-6 sm:p-8"><h2 className="text-2xl font-semibold text-white">{heading}</h2><p className="mt-3 leading-7 text-slate-300">{text}</p></section>)}</div>{page.articles && <div className="space-y-4"><h2 className="text-2xl font-semibold text-white">All insights</h2><ul className="space-y-3">{page.articles.map(([to, label]) => <li key={to}><Link className="text-sky-300 underline hover:text-white" to={to}>{label}</Link></li>)}</ul></div>}
    </article>
  </>
}

function LegalPage({ title, children }) {
  return <><Seo title={`${title} | Pondco Online`} description={`${title} for Pondco Online.`} /><article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 py-12 sm:py-20"><h1 className="text-4xl font-bold text-white">{title}</h1><div className="space-y-5 leading-7 text-slate-300">{children}</div></article></>
}

function NotFound() { return <LegalPage title="Page not found"><p>The page you requested is not published. Return to the <Link className="text-sky-300 underline" to="/">home page</Link>.</p></LegalPage> }

function Layout() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false)
  const location = useLocation()
  useEffect(() => setOpen(false), [location.pathname])
  const links = [['Home', '/'], ['Topics', '/services'], ['Context', '/markets'], ['FAQ', '/faq'], ['Insights', '/insights']]
  return <div className="min-h-screen bg-slate-950 text-slate-100"><header className="border-b border-slate-800 bg-slate-950/95"><nav className="mx-auto flex min-h-16 max-w-6xl items-center justify-between px-4 sm:px-6" aria-label="Primary navigation"><Link className="font-bold tracking-wide text-white" to="/">PONDCO <span className="text-sky-300">ONLINE</span></Link><button className="inline-flex min-h-11 min-w-11 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-slate-700 text-white md:hidden" type="button" aria-expanded={open} aria-controls="mobile-nav" onClick={() => setOpen(!open)}>{open ? <X aria-hidden="true" /> : <Menu aria-hidden="true" />}<span className="sr-only">Menu</span></button><div className="hidden items-center gap-5 md:flex">{links.map(([label, to]) => <Link className="text-sm text-slate-300 hover:text-white focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-sky-300" key={to} to={to}>{label}</Link>)}</div></nav>{open && <div id="mobile-nav" className="border-t border-slate-800 px-4 py-3 md:hidden">{links.map(([label, to]) => <Link className="block min-h-11 py-3 text-slate-200" key={to} to={to}>{label}</Link>)}</div>}</header><main className="px-4 sm:px-6"><Routes>{Object.entries(pages).map(([path, page]) => <Route key={path} path={path} element={<ContentPage page={page} />} />)}<Route path="/about" element={<LegalPage title="About Pondco Online"><p>Pondco Online is an independent information resource publishing practical, plainly written background material on aviation-facility planning and delivery. It exists for people who need clear context on complex airport projects: airport staff evaluating a capital program, consultants and contractors getting oriented, students learning how public infrastructure works, and members of the public trying to understand a project in their community.</p><p><strong>What we publish</strong></p><p>Our material covers the full arc of an aviation-facility project: defining the operating problem, scoping the work, feasibility and planning, design coordination across disciplines, procurement and delivery methods, construction on active airfields, commissioning, and closeout. We also publish guidance on the surrounding context — public accountability, stakeholder coordination, funding programs, and how to verify project claims against primary sources.</p><p>Everything here is written to be read by non-specialists without dumbing down the subject. We explain terms when they first appear, we show how the pieces connect, and we point readers toward the authoritative records — owner documents, procurement notices, permits, and agency publications — that real decisions are based on.</p><p><strong>What we are not</strong></p><p>Pondco Online is an educational publisher, not an engineering firm, law firm, or procurement advisor. Nothing on this site is professional advice. Decisions affecting an active facility should be made with the appropriate licensed professionals and the public authorities that have jurisdiction. We say this on every page because it matters: background knowledge helps you ask better questions, but it does not replace qualified judgment applied to your specific situation.</p><p>We are also not a news outlet. We do not report on individual projects as they happen, and we do not publish project announcements, rankings, or endorsements. Our focus is durable background knowledge — the kind of material that stays useful regardless of which project is in the headlines.</p><p><strong>How the material is produced</strong></p><p>Articles are researched from public records, agency guidance, and established industry practice, then written in plain language and reviewed for accuracy before publication. When we describe a process — how grant funding works, how a delivery method distributes risk, how commissioning is structured — we describe the general pattern, note where practice varies by jurisdiction, and direct readers to the governing documents for their specific case.</p><p>We correct errors when we find them. If you spot something wrong or outdated, contact us and we will investigate and fix it.</p><p><strong>Operation</strong></p><p>Pondco Online is operated by 3000 Studios with supporting delivery partners. Editorial decisions — what to publish, how to frame it, and when to correct it — are made independently of any advertising on the site. Advertising, where present, is served by third-party networks and does not influence content.</p><p><strong>Editorial standards</strong></p><p>Every article states its limits. When a process varies by jurisdiction — procurement rules, permit timelines, funding eligibility — we say so rather than presenting one state&#x27;s practice as universal. When a number is an industry rule of thumb rather than a measured value, we label it as such. And when we do not know something, we say that too, and point to where the answer can be found. A knowledge base earns trust one accurate, carefully bounded claim at a time.</p><p><strong>How to use the knowledge base</strong></p><p>Start with the topic pages if you are new to airport projects — they lay out the phases and the vocabulary. Use the insights articles when you need depth on a specific subject, from reading a master plan to managing closeout. Use the FAQ for quick answers, and the guide to evaluating project information whenever you need to check a claim against the record. The material is organized so that each piece stands alone but the whole forms a coherent introduction to how aviation facilities get planned and built.</p><p><strong>Contact</strong></p><p>For questions, corrections, or topic suggestions: info@pondco.online. See the contact page for details.</p></LegalPage>} /><Route path="/contact" element={<LegalPage title="Contact"><p>The best way to reach us is by email: info@pondco.online. We read everything and reply to substantive inquiries as promptly as we can.</p><p><strong>What to send us</strong></p><p>Corrections. If you find an error in our material — a wrong term, an outdated process description, a broken reference — tell us exactly which page and which passage is wrong, and what the correct information is. Corrections backed by a primary source (an agency publication, a standard, an official record) are the fastest to verify and fix.</p><p>Topic suggestions. We prioritize topics that help readers evaluate real aviation-facility projects: planning, scoping, delivery methods, funding, construction practice, and verification of project claims. Tell us what you are trying to understand and why; the more specific the suggestion, the more useful it is.</p><p>Source pointers. If you know of a public record, agency guide, or dataset that our readers should know about, send the link with a sentence about what it covers. We regularly fold reader-suggested sources into our articles.</p><p>Partnership and enterprise inquiries. For questions about enterprise use, partnerships, or technical matters regarding the site, use the same address with a clear subject line describing the inquiry.</p><p><strong>What we cannot do</strong></p><p>We cannot provide engineering, legal, procurement, or safety advice about a specific project — by email or otherwise. If your question is really &quot;what should we do about our runway/taxiway/terminal,&quot; you need the licensed professionals and authorities with jurisdiction over your facility, not a knowledge base. We will politely decline and suggest where to look instead.</p><p>We also cannot confirm or investigate claims about specific active projects from email alone; verification requires access to the primary records, which we describe in our guide to evaluating project information.</p><p><strong>Before you write</strong></p><p>Many questions are already answered on the site. The FAQ covers common questions about airport planning, funding, and project delivery; the topic pages explain the phases of a project; and the insights articles go deep on subjects like delivery methods, grant funding, and commissioning. A quick search of the site first will usually get you an answer faster than an email exchange.</p><p><strong>Research and media inquiries</strong></p><p>If you are a researcher, student, or journalist working with our material, you are welcome to cite it with attribution and a link. For questions about our sources or methodology behind a specific article, include the article title and the passage in question — that lets us give you a precise answer rather than a general one. We cannot provide interviews on short deadlines, but we do respond to substantive written questions.</p><p><strong>What happens after you send a message</strong></p><p>Substantive messages are read by the editorial side of the operation. Corrections are verified against primary sources before any change is published; if we cannot verify a claimed error, we will tell you what we found rather than making the change blindly. Topic suggestions are logged and weighed against our editorial priorities — we cannot respond to every suggestion individually, but the good ones shape the publishing calendar. Partnership inquiries receive a reply if there is a fit; unsolicited sales pitches do not.</p><p><strong>Response times</strong></p><p>We aim to acknowledge substantive messages within a few business days. Correction reports are triaged first. We do not operate a phone line or a public office for site inquiries; email is the channel that reaches the editorial side of the operation.</p><p><strong>Privacy note</strong></p><p>If you email us, we will use your message and address only to respond and, where relevant, to improve the site. We do not sell contact information or add correspondents to marketing lists. See the privacy policy for details.</p></LegalPage>} /><Route path="/privacy" element={<LegalPage title="Privacy Policy"><p>Last updated: September 2026</p><p>Pondco Online is committed to protecting the privacy of visitors. This policy explains what information is collected when you use the site, how it is used, and the choices you have.</p><p><strong>Information we collect</strong></p><p>Pondco Online is a reading resource. We do not require accounts, and we do not ask for personal information to access articles. If you contact us by email at info@pondco.online, we receive your message and email address, which we use only to respond and, where relevant, to improve the site. We do not sell contact information and we do not add correspondents to marketing lists.</p><p>Like most websites, our hosting infrastructure may process standard technical data — such as IP addresses and request logs — for security, reliability, and troubleshooting. This data is used to operate and protect the site, not to build profiles of readers.</p><p><strong>Third-party advertising and Google AdSense</strong></p><p>We use third-party advertising companies, including Google AdSense, to serve advertisements when you visit our website. These companies may use cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this website or other sites on the Internet.</p><p>• Google&#x27;s use of advertising cookies enables it and its partners to serve ads based on visits to our site and other sites on the web.</p><p>• Users may opt out of personalized advertising by visiting Google Ads Settings or by visiting www.aboutads.info.</p><p>• You can also control cookies through your browser settings; see our cookie notice for details.</p><p>Advertising is served by these third parties under their own privacy policies. Pondco Online does not control the data practices of advertising networks beyond the site-level choices described here.</p><p><strong>Data protection</strong></p><p>We implement reasonable administrative and technical measures to protect information we handle, including industry-standard encryption in transit and access controls on site infrastructure. No method of transmission over the Internet is completely secure, and we cannot guarantee absolute security.</p><p><strong>Children&#x27;s privacy</strong></p><p>This site publishes general educational material and is not directed at children. We do not knowingly collect personal information from children.</p><p><strong>Changes to this policy</strong></p><p>If we change this policy, we will update the &quot;Last updated&quot; date above and publish the revised version on this page. Material changes will be described here.</p><p><strong>Contact</strong></p><p>For questions about this policy, contact: info@pondco.online.</p></LegalPage>} /><Route path="/terms" element={<LegalPage title="Terms of Use"><p>Last updated: September 2026</p><p>By accessing Pondco Online, you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, please do not use the site.</p><p><strong>Educational purpose; not professional advice</strong></p><p>All content on this site is published for general educational purposes. It is not engineering, architectural, legal, procurement, financial, or safety advice, and it does not create any professional or advisory relationship. Decisions affecting a real aviation facility or construction project must be made with the appropriate licensed professionals and the public authorities having jurisdiction. You are solely responsible for how you use the information here.</p><p><strong>Accuracy</strong></p><p>We research our material from public records, agency guidance, and established practice, and we correct errors when we find them. Even so, processes, regulations, and standards change, and general background material cannot capture every jurisdictional variation. Verify important information against primary sources — the governing contract, the applicable code, the agency publication — before relying on it.</p><p><strong>Acceptable use</strong></p><p>You may read, link to, and share our content for personal and educational purposes. You agree not to: disrupt the site&#x27;s operation or security; scrape the site aggressively in ways that degrade service for others; misrepresent our content as your own; or use the site for any unlawful purpose.</p><p><strong>Intellectual property</strong></p><p>Articles and original material on Pondco Online are the property of the site&#x27;s operator and are protected by applicable intellectual property laws. Brief quotations with attribution and a link are welcome. Republication of substantial portions requires prior permission — contact info@pondco.online.</p><p><strong>Third-party content and advertising</strong></p><p>The site may display advertising served by third-party networks, including Google AdSense, and may link to external websites. We do not control third-party sites, products, or advertising content, and we are not responsible for them. Advertising does not constitute an endorsement.</p><p><strong>Limitation of liability</strong></p><p>To the maximum extent permitted by law, Pondco Online and its operator are not liable for any damages arising from your use of, or inability to use, the site or its content. The site is provided &quot;as is&quot; without warranties of any kind, express or implied.</p><p><strong>Changes</strong></p><p>We may update these terms from time to time. Continued use of the site after changes are published constitutes acceptance of the revised terms. The &quot;Last updated&quot; date above reflects the most recent revision.</p><p><strong>Contact</strong></p><p>Questions about these terms: info@pondco.online.</p></LegalPage>} /><Route path="/cookies" element={<LegalPage title="Cookie Notice"><p>Last updated: September 2026</p><p>This notice explains the cookies and similar technologies used on Pondco Online, why they are used, and how you can control them.</p><p><strong>What cookies are</strong></p><p>Cookies are small text files stored on your device by your browser. They can remember preferences, keep a session working, measure usage, or — in the case of advertising cookies — help show ads relevant to your interests. Similar technologies, such as local storage and tracking pixels, work in comparable ways.</p><p><strong>Cookies we use</strong></p><p>Strictly necessary cookies. Essential hosting and security services may use strictly necessary technical mechanisms to provide and protect the site — for example, load balancing, security filtering, and remembering a consent choice. These are required for the site to function and cannot be disabled through site settings.</p><p>Advertising cookies. We use third-party advertising services, including Google AdSense, which may place cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this site and other sites on the Internet. Google&#x27;s advertising cookies enable Google and its partners to select and measure ads. These cookies are set by the advertising providers, not by Pondco Online, and are governed by the providers&#x27; own policies.</p><p>Analytics. If we enable web analytics in the future, we will update this notice to describe what is measured and offer an appropriate choice.</p><p><strong>Your choices</strong></p><p>You have several ways to control cookies:</p><p>• Browser settings. All major browsers let you block or delete cookies, either entirely or per site. Blocking strictly necessary cookies may impair site functionality; blocking advertising cookies will generally result in less relevant ads rather than no ads.</p><p>• Google Ads Settings. You can opt out of Google&#x27;s personalized advertising at Google Ads Settings.</p><p>• Industry opt-outs. You can opt out of interest-based advertising from participating companies at www.aboutads.info.</p><p><strong>Changes to this notice</strong></p><p>If our use of cookies changes — for example, if we add analytics or new advertising providers — we will update this notice and the &quot;Last updated&quot; date above, and provide an appropriate consent choice where required by law.</p><p><strong>Contact</strong></p><p>Questions about this notice: info@pondco.online.</p></LegalPage>} /><Route path="*" element={<NotFound />} /></Routes></main><footer className="border-t border-slate-800"><div className="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-4 px-4 py-8 text-sm text-slate-400 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between sm:px-6"><p>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Pondco Online</p><div className="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2"><Link to="/about">About</Link><Link to="/contact">Contact</Link><Link to="/privacy">Privacy</Link><Link to="/terms">Terms</Link><Link to="/cookies">Cookies</Link></div></div></footer></div>
}

export default function App() { return <BrowserRouter><Layout /></BrowserRouter> }
